@format 1
@id media-removal.common.workspace-media-clear
@title Workspace media clear
@section Media removal
@area Common
@entry workspace-clear
@summary Trace PWorkspaceMediaClear in its actual order and return semantics: force-close viewer, clear flow, conditionally call Files clear based on any listed path, then clear Merge groups.
@related media-removal.common.files-clear, media-removal.common.docket-removal, media-preview.common.viewer-close, media-preview.common.media-publication, media-removal.in-global-interface, media-removal.in-worklist
@tag media
@tag removal
@tag common
@tag clear

@alias workspace = Cadroue.UIShell.PControlBar.PWorkspace
@alias viewer = Cadroue.UIShell.PPanels.PViewer
@alias flow = Cadroue.UIShell.PFlow.PFlow
@alias list = Cadroue.UIShell.PPanels.PList
@alias group = Cadroue.UIShell.PPanels.PGroup

[workspace-clear] Workspace media-clear request <input>
~ This starts with workspace media-clear request.
[Technical explanation]
- Enter PWorkspaceMediaClear for one workspace and initialize its changed-result from the force-close viewer result @ workspace.PWorkspaceMediaClear(...)
> close-viewer

[close-viewer] Force-close workspace viewer <process>
~ This step handles force-close workspace viewer.
[Technical explanation]
- Force-close the workspace viewer when present; the detailed backend-unload, viewer-ownership, committed-state, and empty-cargo sequence is Media preview / Viewer close @ viewer.PViewerMediaClose(...)
! See Media preview / Viewer close for the LMediaUnload event that is raised before the viewer's own load serial is advanced.
> clear-flow

[clear-flow] Clear workspace flow <process>
~ This step handles clear workspace flow.
[Technical explanation]
- Clear the workspace flow after the viewer-close attempt and OR that result into the workspace changed-result @ flow.PFlowClear(...)
> list-check

[list-check] Any Files path listed <decision>
~ The program checks any Files path listed.
[Technical explanation]
- Test the full Files path list, including locked entries; PWorkspaceMediaClear calls PListClear whenever at least one path is listed @ workspace.PWorkspaceMediaClear(...)
? "One or more Files paths listed" > clear-list
? "No Files paths" > no-list

[clear-list] Clear unlocked Files entries <process>
~ This step handles clear unlocked Files entries.
[Technical explanation]
- Call PListClear; it submits only unlocked docket paths for shared Docket removal, so locked Files entries can remain after this call @ list.PListClear(...)
! See Media removal / Files clear and Media removal / Docket removal for the synchronous list-removal details.
> report-list-clear

[no-list] No Files clear requested <junction>
> no-list-align

[report-list-clear] Mark workspace changed from Files precondition <storage>
~ The program saves mark workspace changed from Files precondition.
[Technical explanation]
- Set the workspace changed-result true because the Files list was nonempty before PListClear, regardless of whether PListClear actually found an unlocked path to remove @ workspace.PWorkspaceMediaClear(...)
> after-list

[no-list-align] No-Files alignment <junction>
> after-list

[after-list] Files branch convergence <junction>
> clear-group

[clear-group] Clear Merge grouping state <process>
~ This step handles clear Merge grouping state.
[Technical explanation]
- Clear the workspace's Merge group state when such a group surface exists; this operation does not contribute to PWorkspaceMediaClear's returned changed-result @ group.PGroupClear(...)
> workspace-result

[workspace-result] Workspace media-clear result <output>
~ This path ends with workspace media-clear result.
[Technical explanation]
= Viewer and flow clearing have been attempted, unlocked Files entries are removed when present, locked Files entries may remain, and Merge grouping is cleared. The returned Boolean can be true solely because Files paths existed before PListClear, even if every listed entry was locked.
